#N canvas 20 20 2520 1100 12;
#X text 20 20 alert-buffer-probe -- answers the last unknown in plan-display.md: can a patch draw into the ALERT buffer (screen 4) and show it with setscreen? Only setscreen itself is documented \, from save-patch.sh. Drawing into buffer 4 and flipping it has never been tested., f 90;
#X text 20 110 Load it as a third patch after mother.pd and main.pd. It leaves the running patch alone: g_oled keeps drawing to screen 3 throughout \, and this only changes which buffer is SHOWN., f 90;
#X obj 20 210 loadbang;
#X obj 20 260 del 4000;
#X obj 20 300 t b b b;
#X msg 1400 350 drawing into buffer 4 then switching to it;
#X obj 1400 390 print PROBE;
#X msg 620 440 sendtyped /oled/gClear ii 4 1  \, sendtyped /oled/gBox iiiiii 4 0 0 127 63 1  \, sendtyped /oled/gPrintln iiiiis 4 8 16 16 1 BUFFER-4  \, sendtyped /oled/gPrintln iiiiis 4 8 44 8 1 if-you-see-this-it-works  \, sendtyped /oled/gFlip i 4  \, sendtyped /oled/setscreen i 4;
#X obj 620 490 s oscOut;
#X obj 20 580 del 6000;
#X obj 20 620 t b b;
#X msg 1400 670 switching back to screen 3 -- the meters should return;
#X obj 1400 710 print PROBE;
#X msg 620 760 sendtyped /oled/setscreen i 3;
#X obj 620 800 s oscOut;
#X text 20 870 WHAT THE RESULT MEANS. If BUFFER-4 appears for six seconds and then the meters come back \, buffer 4 works -- and g_oled's alert layer could move there \, dropping from about 70 messages a second while an alert is up to five per alert. If the screen goes blank or never changes \, the inference was wrong and drawing alerts on screen 3 was the right call. Either way record it in plan-display.md: it is the last unknown in the OLED section., f 90;
